WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.500
Peppered moths rest on trees by day.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.120
Some are pale. Some are nearly black.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:17.240
In smoky industrial areas, soot darkened the trees, and dark moths became common.

4
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:25.280
Later the air got cleaner, and dark moths became rare again, in Britain and in parts of the United States.

5
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.320
No moth changed its colour.

6
00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.500
So why do some traits become more common over time?

7
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:36.383
The moths in one wood are a population.

8
00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:42.043
A population is a group of the same kind of living thing in one area.

9
00:00:43.223 --> 00:00:45.723
Members of a population are not all the same.

10
00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:48.803
Some moths are pale and some are dark.

11
00:00:49.903 --> 00:00:50.903
Colour is inherited.

12
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:54.683
In this model, young moths have their parents' colour.

13
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:57.173
Now add the environment.

14
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:04.113
The environment is everything around a living thing, such as bark, air, food and

15
00:01:04.113 --> 00:01:07.893
predators. Here the bark is dark and sooty.

16
00:01:08.673 --> 00:01:11.533
Birds spot more of the moths that stand out from the bark.

17
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:14.973
On this bark, the pale moths stand out.

18
00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:18.233
So birds find more pale moths than dark ones.

19
00:01:19.293 --> 00:01:20.913
Eaten moths have no young.

20
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:25.273
The moths that are not eaten live to reproduce.

21
00:01:26.253 --> 00:01:28.373
To reproduce means to have young.

22
00:01:29.193 --> 00:01:30.953
Their young inherit their colour.

23
00:01:32.053 --> 00:01:36.133
Most of the survivors here are dark, so most of the young are dark.

24
00:01:37.193 --> 00:01:40.193
One step from parents to young is a generation.

25
00:01:41.117 --> 00:01:43.577
Repeat that for many generations.

26
00:01:44.297 --> 00:01:47.457
The colour that matched the bark becomes more common.

27
00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:52.657
How common a trait is in a population is called trait frequency.

28
00:01:53.757 --> 00:01:57.817
In this sketch, the share of dark moths grows each generation.

29
00:01:58.957 --> 00:02:01.457
Natural selection shifts trait frequency.

30
00:02:01.977 --> 00:02:03.897
It does not change any one moth.

31
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:06.787
This is natural selection.

32
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:12.227
Individuals with traits that suit their environment are more likely to survive and

33
00:02:12.227 --> 00:02:18.407
reproduce. A trait shaped this way, that helps an organism survive and reproduce, is

34
00:02:18.407 --> 00:02:23.027
an adaptation. Dark colour on sooty bark was an adaptation.

35
00:02:23.687 --> 00:02:26.507
On clean, pale bark, the same colour was not.

36
00:02:27.487 --> 00:02:29.867
So why do some traits become more common?

37
00:02:30.887 --> 00:02:34.547
Individuals with those traits survive and reproduce more often.
